{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And Israel dwelt in the land of Egypt, in the land of Goshen.</b> The region of Goshen was close to Egypt, and because his twelve sons were each distinguished and honored, each of them fit to stand before kings, therefore Jacob wished to settle in the land of Goshen and not in Egypt proper, where the seat of royalty was, lest they be made of them treasurers and officials." "<b>And it appears</b> by way of the plain sense (peshat) of this verse: since Jacob our father knew that their descent to Egypt was the cause of the exile for his seed, and that it would now begin with him, therefore it says וישב ישראל — 'and Israel settled' — that is, he took up residence there, not according to the intention of his sons who said to Pharaoh לגור בארץ באנו — 'to sojourn in the land we have come' — for they thought to return to the land of Canaan as soon as the famine ended, and therefore they said לגור, 'to sojourn,' and not to settle." "And it says <b>and they took hold in it.</b> From the root meaning of אחוזה, holding and inheritance — and their intention was not fulfilled. And it should have said ויאחזו בניו בה, 'and his sons took hold in it,' but it is from the language of the root that is grasped in the ground, from which the branches extend and ויפרו וירבו מאד — 'and they were fruitful and multiplied exceedingly' — and therefore Scripture did not need to specify, but said simply ויאחזו, 'and they took hold,' for since it already mentioned ישראל, who is the root, there is no doubt that the branches take hold from the root." "<b>And behold,</b> the righteous — Scripture likens them to a root that fills the whole earth, as it is written (Psalms 80): ״פנית לפניה ותשרש שרשיה ותמלא ארץ״, and it is written (Isaiah 27): ״הבאים ישרש יעקב״. And therefore you find in this parashah: ״ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים מצרימה״ — 'the coming' — whereas regarding the wicked, Scripture likens them to a tree that has no root, for they have no permanence or standing; thus you find concerning Sennacherib the wicked (Ezekiel 31): ״הנה אשור ארז בלבנון יפה ענף וחרש מצל״. And likewise concerning Nebuchadnezzar it is written (Daniel 4): ״רבא אילנא ותקיף״. Scripture mentioned the height of the tree and its boundaries and its branches and its fruit, but did not mention in it any root in the account of the dream — only what the angel said: ״ברם עקר שרשוהי בארעא שבוקו״, and this was to hint to him that his kingship would return to him, which is what Daniel said: ״ודי אמרו למשבק עקר שרשוהי די אילנא מלכותך לך קימא״. And so Scripture said concerning the wicked (Isaiah 40): ״אף בל נטעו אף בל זורעו אף בל שרש בארץ גזעם״, and it is written (Hosea 9): ״שרשם יבש פרי בל יעשון״. But the righteous — they have a root, and their root endures forever, never wavering; thus Solomon said (Proverbs 12): ״לא יכון אדם ברשע ושורש צדיקים בל ימוט״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:04.888211627Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}